 mixed {110}+{100} prism. Rows run from {101} to {111} terminations.","row_title":"Termination","col_title":"Prism","rows":[{"label":"T1","detail":"{101} only"},{"label":"T2","detail":"{111} < {101}"},{"label":"T3","detail":"{111} > {101}"},{"label":"T4","detail":"{111} only"}],"cols":[{"label":"P1","detail":"{110} only"},{"label":"P2","detail":"{110} > {100}"},{"label":"P3","detail":"{110} > {100}"},{"label":"P4","detail":"{110} < {100}"}],"note":""},"about":"\n<p>Rutile is tetragonal like zircon, but its habit is dominated by the {110} prism with a subordinate {100} prism and\n{101} and {111} dipyramid terminations. Rutile ranges from equant to acicular, including needles exsolved in garnet\nand quartz. Rutile U–Pb and Zr-in-rutile thermometry are central to metamorphic petrochronology. Grain size and shape\nset the Pb diffusion domain. No Pupin-style typology for rutile has been published, so this grid uses Pupin's\nprism × termination layout on the rutile cell.</p>\n","habits":[{"name":"Prismatic","text":"m{110} (± a{100}) elongated along c, with e{101} and/or s{111} terminations."},{"name":"Acicular / needles","text":"Extreme m elongation. Sagenite in quartz and oriented exsolution needles in garnet."},{"name":"Equant / dipyramidal","text":"e + s with a short prism."},{"name":"Geniculate twins (not modelled)","text":"Elbow twins on {101} and reticulate twins on {301} are not convex."}],"references":[{"citation":"Zack, T., Kooijman, E. (2017). Petrology and geochronology of rutile. <i>Reviews in Mineralogy and Geochemistry</i> 83, 443–467.","doi":"10.2138/rmg.2017.83.14","url":null,"note":"The standard review of rutile petrochronology.","tags":["review"]},{"citation":"Meinhold, G. (2010). Rutile and its applications in earth sciences. <i>Earth-Science Reviews</i> 102, 1–28.","doi":"10.1016/j.earscirev.2010.06.001","url":null,"note":"Covers occurrence, habit and provenance.","tags":["review","morphology"]},{"citation":"Cherniak, D.J. (2000). Pb diffusion in rutile. <i>Contributions to Mineralogy and Petrology</i> 139, 198–207.","doi":"10.1007/PL00007671","url":null,"note":"Diffusion data for grain-size and closure modelling.","tags":["diffusion"]},{"citation":"Kooijman, E., Mezger, K., Berndt, J. (2010). Constraints on the U–Pb systematics of metamorphic rutile from in situ LA-ICP-MS analysis. <i>Earth and Planetary Science Letters</i> 293, 321–330.","doi":"10.1016/j.epsl.2010.02.047","url":null,"note":"Grain-scale U–Pb profiles used to study diffusion and closure.","tags":["geochronology","geometry"]},{"citation":"Ague, J.J., Eckert, J.O. (2012). Precipitation of rutile and ilmenite needles in garnet: implications for extreme metamorphic conditions in the Acadian Orogen, U.S.A. <i>American Mineralogist</i> 97, 840–855.","doi":"10.2138/am.2012.4015","url":null,"note":"Links needle morphology and orientation to ultrahigh-temperature conditions.","tags":["morphology"]},{"citation":"Proyer, A. et al. (2013). TiO₂ exsolution from garnet by open-system precipitation: evidence from crystallographic and shape preferred orientation of rutile inclusions. <i>Contributions to Mineralogy and Petrology</i> 166, 211–234.","doi":"10.1007/s00410-013-0872-7","url":null,"note":"Rutile inclusion shape and orientation as a record of the precipitation process.","tags":["morphology"]},{"citation":"Swope, R.J., Smyth, J.R., Larson, A.C. (1995). H in rutile-type compounds: I. Single-crystal neutron and X-ray diffraction study of H in rutile. <i>American Mineralogist</i> 80, 448–453.","doi":"10.2138/am-1995-5-604","url":null,"note":"Rutile structure refinement.","tags":["lattice"]}],"cells":[{"id":"P1_T1","row":0,"col":0,"label":"P1·T1","description":"Prism: {110} only. Termination: {101} only.","default":false,"params":{"face_distances":{"110":1.0,"100":null,"101":0.993098,"111":null},"cap_translation":3.768488,"scale":0.30298106,"planes":{"110":0.30298106,"101":1.26079781}},"fractions":{"110":0.8777,"101":0.1223},"metrics":{"volume":1.0,"surface_